Tuberculosis (Pulmonary)
Respiratory System
Chronic cough >3 weeks, Drenching night sweats, Unexplained weight loss, Apical cavitation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-048
Reason for Visit: Worsening chronic cough now producing blood-streaked sputum, accompanied by profound weight loss and drenching night sweats, requiring urgent isolation and assessment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with a weeks-long chronic cough, significant unintentional weight loss, and new-onset hemoptysis. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to evaluate for active, highly infectious pulmonary tuberculosis and establish strict immediate airborne isolation protocols prior to the regular in-hours clinic opening.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I've been coughing for a month and losing weight rapidly, but this morning I coughed up a spoonful of bright red blood and I'm terrified.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have been progressing insidiously over the past 5 weeks. The patient reports a chronic, persistent cough lasting longer than three weeks, initially dry but now involving the expectoration of purulent and blood-streaked sputum. They report profound fatigue, malaise, and unexplained, progressive weight loss (7 kg) alongside severe anorexia. The patient experiences a low-grade evening fever and drenching night sweats that require changing their sleepwear. They describe mild pleuritic chest pain localized to the upper back.
Functional Status: Independent but profoundly fatigued and visibly cachectic.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living in a shared household (roommates instructed to wait outside during the consult); high risk of community transmission.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 38.2 C | RR: 20 breaths/min | SpO2: 96% on room air | Wt: 58 Kg (Significant recent decline)
General: Cachectic, chronically ill appearance, flushed, coughing frequently into a tissue.
Physical Exam: Chest auscultation reveals distinct post-tussive apical crackles and bronchial breath sounds localized to the right upper lobe, highly suspicious for apical consolidation or cavitation. Remainder of the lung fields are relatively clear. Palpation reveals mild cervical lymphadenopathy. The clinical tetrad of a chronic cough >3 weeks, drenching night sweats, unexplained weight loss, and apical signs is virtually pathognomonic for active pulmonary TB.
Environmental Observations: Poorly ventilated bedroom; sputum-soiled tissues in the wastebasket.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications providing relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Tuberculosis (Pulmonary) - Status: Suspected (Highly Infectious)
Differentials:

Lung Malignancy (NSCLC) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of prominent infectious signs (drenching night sweats, daily evening fevers, purulent sputum), though malignancy remains a crucial differential if TB testing is negative.

Lung Abscess (Non-TB) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Sputum is not putrid/foul-smelling, and the classic apical localization (rather than dependent lower lobes typical of aspiration).

Chronic Bronchitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The profound systemic toxicity (weight loss, severe night sweats) and the focal apical crackles rather than widespread diffuse rhonchi.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Critical (Public Health Risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits the textbook clinical phenotype for active, infectious pulmonary Tuberculosis: a chronic cough exceeding 3 weeks, new hemoptysis, drenching night sweats, profound weight loss, and localized apical crackles. The presence of hemoptysis strongly suggests advancing cavitary lung disease. Delaying clinical assessment until the next in-hours period would have permitted continued, unchecked airborne exposure to household contacts and the community. An urgent after-hours home visit was strictly required to confirm the clinical suspicion, immediately enforce strict airborne isolation, and arrange a rapid public health diagnostic pathway (sputum AFB and urgent chest X-ray).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided a surgical mask for the patient to wear constantly when interacting with others. Provided three sterile sputum specimen jars for urgent acid-fast bacilli (AFB) smears and Mycobacterium culture. Generated an urgent referral for a chest X-ray and notified the local Public Health Unit of a highly suspected case.
Positive Behavioral Change: Open the windows in your bedroom as wide as possible and keep the door closed to the rest of the house; physically increasing the ventilation actively blows the infectious bacteria outside, directly protecting your roommates from catching the disease.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Mandatory Public Health nurse follow-up required.
DME/Safety (Optional): N95 masks provided for any unavoidable close contacts.
Follow-up: The patient must drop off the first sputum sample to pathology immediately in the morning and proceed directly to the X-ray facility (wearing a mask); GP/Public Health review within 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer (call 000) to the Emergency Department if the coughing brings up more than a tablespoon of fresh red blood, if you develop severe shortness of breath at rest, or if you become confused.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This diagnosis requires urgent laboratory confirmation via sputum testing and an X-ray; until these results are negative, you must be treated as highly infectious.
Clinical Course: If confirmed, treatment requires taking a specific combination of specialized antibiotics for at least 6 months without missing a single dose.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not leave your house, use public transport, or go to work under any circumstances until the health department clears you.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A slow-growing, highly contagious bacteria has nested in the top of your lung, slowly destroying the tissue and causing the night sweats and bleeding; we must test your sputum immediately to prove it is this specific bacteria so we can start the correct 6-month cure.
Actionable Steps: You must spit your very first, deepest cough of the morning into the sterile jar on three consecutive days; the bacteria multiply overnight, so this exact morning sample is the only way the lab can quickly confirm the diagnosis and trigger your treatment.

